detail.abstract:
Background: The global poly-crises presents a multifaceted challenge from both environmental and social perspectives. Next to other consequences, they affect physical and mental health. Climate-related stress and anxiety have become an increasingly significant issue today. Research objective: This study's research objective was to examine climate anxiety among urban residents and the potential mitigating role of urban green spaces in two European cities with focus on urban heat waves and the role of UGS in adaptation to them.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Methodological approach: The study is based upon empirical data, obtained via online panel surveys in Vienna, Austria (n = 1000) and Milan, Italy (n = 512) at the end of 2024 and beginning of 2025. A central element of the study was the use of the Perceived Stress Scale Test PSS-10 as a Pre-Event Baseline Test, and later to measure the Post-Event Stress as a Stress Level during an Extreme Weather Event. Additionally, a random component was used to measure the impact of Urban Green Spaces on stress. For this, two random samples answered the Questionnaire in a different order. Selected Key Findings: While Milanese respondents reported significantly higher levels of climate-related worry, Viennese participants perceived themselves as more frequently exposed to heatwaves, despite meteorological data indicating a bit higher actual heat burden in Milan. This divergence points to the importance of subjective risk perception, which may be shaped by local adaptation processes, cultural coping practices, and differences in urban infrastructure as well as UGS. The findings of this study highlight the relationship between urban green spaces and climate anxiety in urban populations, as well as their potential to serve as a transformative capacity in the transition toward sustainable futures. Overall, the results indicate that green spaces can contribute to stress reduction during heatwaves and serve as valuable environments for general emotional and mental restoration. In Vienna, respondents tended to view green spaces as integral to cope with extreme heat, assigning high emotional and functional value to their proximity, shade, and water features. In Milan, however, while specific features such as shading, quietness, and lack of crowding were highly valued, a larger share of respondents reported avoiding green spaces during heatwaves due to excessive heat.&nbsp;
</div>
